{"parentUuid":"887b028f-11bf-49a7-b77c-8aefac8a55f9","isSidechain":false,"userType":"external","cwd":"/Users/soph/Work/entire/devenv/cli","sessionId":"52c38054-a209-4b88-9a0d-d98ba15865dd","version":"2.1.3","gitBranch":"main","slug":"vectorized-weaving-quail","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-5-20250929","id":"msg_014QFkHeYUdMboTAdy7x9A6v","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01JWVNYBVqrdtjcq1RxbGbLa","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/Users/soph/Work/entire/devenv/cli/docs/opencode-integration-findings.md","content":"# OpenCode Integration Analysis: Findings\n\n**Date:** 2026-01-10\n**Author:** Claude (Sonnet 4.5)\n**Purpose:** Technical analysis of integrating OpenCode with the Entire CLI\n\n---\n\n## Executive Summary\n\nThis document analyzes the feasibility of integrating OpenCode (a multi-agent TypeScript/JavaScript CLI) with the Entire CLI (a Go-based git session management system). After reviewing both codebases, documentation, and architectural patterns, I've identified three viable integration approaches:\n\n1. **OpenCode Plugin Approach** (RECOMMENDED): Write an OpenCode plugin that emits Entire CLI hook events\n2. **Entire Agent Adapter**: Add OpenCode as a new agent type in Entire CLI\n3. **Standalone Bridge Process**: Create a separate process that monitors OpenCode events and triggers Entire hooks\n\n---\n\n## System Architecture Analysis\n\n### OpenCode Architecture\n\n**Core Components:**\n- **Language:** TypeScript/JavaScript (Bun runtime)\n- **Plugin System:** Event-driven architecture with synchronous hook execution\n- **Event Bus:** Centralized pub/sub system using Zod schemas for type safety\n- **Session Management:** Built-in session lifecycle with compaction and resumption\n- **Multi-Agent Support:** Native support for spawning and coordinating multiple agents\n\n**Plugin System Details:**\n```typescript\n// Plugins export functions that return hook implementations\nexport const MyPlugin: Plugin = async ({ project, client, $, directory, worktree }) => {\n  return {\n    \"tool.execute.before\": async (input, output) => { /* ... */ },\n    \"tool.execute.after\": async (input, output) => { /* ... */ },\n    \"session.created\": async (input) => { /* ... */ },\n    // ... other hooks\n  }\n}\n```\n\n**Event Types Available:**\n- Session: `created`, `updated`, `deleted`, `idle`, `status`, `compacted`, `diff`, `error`\n- Tool: `execute.before`, `execute.after`\n- File: `edited`, `watcher.updated`\n- Message: `updated`, `removed`, `part.updated`, `part.removed`\n- Permission: `replied`, `updated`\n- LSP: `client.diagnostics`, `updated`\n- Command: `executed`\n- Todo: `updated`\n- TUI: `prompt.append`, `command.execute`, `toast.show`\n\n**Plugin Loading:**\n- Global plugins: `~/.config/opencode/plugin/`\n- Project plugins: `.opencode/plugin/`\n- NPM plugins: Specified in `opencode.json`, auto-installed via Bun\n\n**Event Flow:**\n1. OpenCode core publishes events via `Bus.publish()`\n2. Plugin system subscribes to all events via `Bus.subscribeAll()`\n3. Plugins receive events and can modify state or trigger external commands\n4. All plugin hooks run synchronously in sequence\n\n### Entire CLI Architecture\n\n**Core Components:**\n- **Language:** Go 1.25\n- **Git Integration:** Uses go-git library + CLI commands for operations\n- **Strategy Pattern:** Multiple strategies for session/checkpoint storage\n- **Hook System:** Git hooks + agent-specific hooks (Claude Code, potentially others)\n- **Session Management:** Tracks AI agent sessions with metadata and checkpoints\n\n**Hook System Details:**\n\nTwo types of hooks:\n\n1. **Git Hooks** (in `.git/hooks/`):\n   - `prepare-commit-msg`: Adds session metadata to commits\n   - `commit-msg`: Validates commit messages\n   - `post-commit`: Condenses session data\n   - `pre-push`: Pushes session logs alongside code\n\n2. **Agent Hooks** (Claude Code-specific, in `.claude/settings.json`):\n   - `session-start`: Initializes session tracking\n   - `user-prompt-submit`: Records user prompts\n   - `stop`: Saves checkpoint when session ends\n   - `pre-task` (PreToolUse[Task]): Creates checkpoint before subagent spawns\n   - `post-task` (PostToolUse[Task]): Links subagent transcript to checkpoint\n   - `post-todo` (PostToolUse[TodoWrite]): Updates checkpoint descriptions\n\n**Strategy System:**\n\n- **Manual-Commit Strategy** (default): No commits to active branch, uses shadow branches (`entire/<commit-hash>`)\n- **Auto-Commit Strategy**: Creates clean commits on active branch with `Entire-Checkpoint` trailer\n\n**Session Metadata Storage:**\n\nShadow branches (`entire/<commit-hash>`):\n```\n.entire/metadata/<session-id>/\n├── full.jsonl          # Session transcript\n├── prompt.txt          # User prompts\n├── context.md          # Generated context\n└── tasks/<tool-use-id>/\n    ├── checkpoint.json\n    └── agent-<id>.jsonl\n```\n\nMetadata branch (`entire/sessions`):\n```\n<checkpoint-id[:2]>/<checkpoint-id[2:]>/\n├── metadata.json\n├── full.jsonl\n├── prompt.txt\n├── context.md\n└── tasks/<tool-use-id>/\n```\n\n---\n\n## Key Differences & Compatibility Challenges\n\n### 1. Runtime Environment\n- **OpenCode:** JavaScript/TypeScript (Bun)\n- **Entire CLI:** Go\n- **Challenge:** Cannot directly share code or link libraries\n\n### 2. Hook Execution Model\n- **OpenCode:** In-process, synchronous, typed (Zod schemas)\n- **Entire CLI:** External process, async, JSON over stdin/stdout\n- **Compatibility:** Good - OpenCode plugins can spawn external processes\n\n### 3. Session Lifecycle\n- **OpenCode:** Built-in session management with IDs, compaction, resumption\n- **Entire CLI:** External session tracking via git commits and metadata files\n- **Challenge:** Need to bridge session IDs and metadata between systems\n\n### 4. Tool/Hook Mapping\n\nOpenCode tools map reasonably well to Entire CLI hook points:\n\n| OpenCode Event | Entire CLI Hook | Mapping Quality |\n|----------------|-----------------|-----------------|\n| `session.created` | `session-start` | Excellent |\n| `session.idle` / `session.status` | `stop` | Good |\n| `tool.execute.before` (Task) | `pre-task` | Excellent |\n| `tool.execute.after` (Task) | `post-task` | Excellent |\n| `tool.execute.after` (TodoWrite) | `post-todo` | Good |\n| `file.edited` / `tool.execute.after` (Write/Edit) | Git hooks | Medium |\n| User prompt submission | `user-prompt-submit` | Missing - needs workaround |\n\n**Gap:** OpenCode doesn't have a direct \"user prompt submitted\" event. We'd need to infer this from `message.updated` events or use TUI events.\n\n### 5. Metadata Access\n- **OpenCode:** Session data stored in SQLite database, accessible via SDK client\n- **Entire CLI:** Expects file paths to transcripts and metadata\n- **Challenge:** Need to export OpenCode session data to files or provide paths\n\n### 6. Git Integration\n- **OpenCode:** Basic git operations via tools, but no strategy system\n- **Entire CLI:** Deep git integration with shadow branches, metadata branches, trailers\n- **Opportunity:** OpenCode can leverage Entire's sophisticated git session tracking\n\n---\n\n## Integration Approach Comparison\n\n### Option 1: OpenCode Plugin (RECOMMENDED)\n\n**How it works:**\n1. Write a TypeScript plugin for OpenCode: `opencode-entire-integration`\n2. Plugin subscribes to OpenCode events\n3. On relevant events, plugin spawns `entire hooks opencode <hook-name>` commands\n4. Entire CLI reads event data from stdin (JSON), performs git operations, exits\n5. Plugin handles async execution, error handling, logging\n\n**Example Plugin Structure:**\n```typescript\nexport const EntireIntegrationPlugin: Plugin = async ({ project, client, $, directory }) => {\n  return {\n    \"session.created\": async ({ event }) => {\n      await $`entire hooks opencode session-start`.stdin(\"json\", {\n        session_id: event.properties.info.id,\n        session_ref: event.properties.info.id, // OpenCode session ID\n        timestamp: new Date().toISOString(),\n      })\n    },\n\n    \"tool.execute.before\": async (input, output) => {\n      if (input.tool === \"task\") {\n        await $`entire hooks opencode pre-task`.stdin(\"json\", {\n          session_id: input.sessionID,\n          tool_use_id: input.callID,\n          tool_input: output.args,\n          transcript_path: await exportTranscript(input.sessionID),\n        })\n      }\n    },\n\n    \"tool.execute.after\": async (input, output) => {\n      if (input.tool === \"task\") {\n        await $`entire hooks opencode post-task`.stdin(\"json\", {\n          session_id: input.sessionID,\n          tool_use_id: input.callID,\n          tool_response: { agentId: output.metadata?.agentId },\n          transcript_path: await exportTranscript(input.sessionID),\n        })\n      }\n    },\n\n    // ... more hooks\n  }\n}\n```\n\n**Pros:**\n- Clean separation of concerns - OpenCode handles events, Entire handles git\n- Uses OpenCode's native plugin system\n- No changes to OpenCode core needed\n- Can be installed per-project or globally\n- Easy to version and distribute (NPM package)\n- Can evolve independently of both projects\n\n**Cons:**\n- Requires maintaining a TypeScript plugin\n- Need to handle async command execution and error handling\n- Must export/convert OpenCode session data to formats Entire expects\n- Additional dependency for users (plugin must be installed)\n\n**Implementation Complexity:** Medium\n\n---\n\n### Option 2: Entire Agent Adapter\n\n**How it works:**\n1. Add OpenCode as a new agent type in `cmd/entire/cli/agent/`\n2. Implement `Agent` interface with OpenCode-specific session discovery\n3. Hook installation modifies `opencode.json` to add plugin\n4. Either bundle the plugin with Entire CLI or install it as dependency\n\n**Directory Structure:**\n```\ncmd/entire/cli/agent/opencode/\n├── opencode.go           # Agent implementation\n├── hooks.go              # Hook handlers\n├── types.go              # OpenCode-specific types\n├── session_discovery.go  # Find sessions via OpenCode API/DB\n└── plugin/\n    └── entire-plugin.ts  # Bundled plugin code\n```\n\n**Pros:**\n- Consistent with Entire's architecture (similar to claude-code agent)\n- Single installation point (`entire setup`)\n- Can reuse existing strategy and hook infrastructure\n- Native Go error handling and logging\n\n**Cons:**\n- Tighter coupling between projects\n- Need to bundle/manage TypeScript plugin from Go code\n- Requires understanding both codebases to maintain\n- More invasive changes to Entire CLI\n- Plugin must still be installed into OpenCode's plugin directory\n\n**Implementation Complexity:** High\n\n---\n\n### Option 3: Standalone Bridge Process\n\n**How it works:**\n1. Create a separate Go process: `entire-opencode-bridge`\n2. Bridge monitors OpenCode's event stream (via API or file watching)\n3. Translates OpenCode events to Entire hook calls\n4. Runs as daemon or systemd service\n\n**Pros:**\n- Complete decoupling of systems\n- Can run independently of both OpenCode and Entire\n- Easy to debug and monitor\n- Could support multiple agent types in the future\n\n**Cons:**\n- Most complex architecture (three processes instead of two)\n- Need to implement event monitoring (polling/watching)\n- Additional daemon/service management\n- Higher resource usage\n- More failure modes (bridge process could crash)\n- Delayed event processing (not synchronous)\n\n**Implementation Complexity:** Very High\n\n---\n\n## Detailed Technical Considerations\n\n### Session ID Mapping\n\nOpenCode generates ULIDs for session IDs (e.g., `REDACTED`).\nEntire CLI expects session IDs in hooks and stores them in git trailers.\n\n**Solution:** Use OpenCode's session ID directly as `SessionID` in Entire CLI. The session ID is already unique and timestamped.\n\n**SessionRef:** For OpenCode agent, `SessionRef` would be the OpenCode session ID (same as SessionID). This is different from Claude Code which uses file paths.\n\n### Transcript Export\n\nEntire CLI expects transcript paths for checkpoint metadata.\n\n**Options:**\n\n1. **Export on demand**: Plugin exports transcript to `.entire/opencode-sessions/<session-id>/transcript.jsonl` when hooks are triggered\n   - Pros: Only exports when needed\n   - Cons: Extra I/O on every hook call\n\n2. **Watch and sync**: Plugin watches session updates and maintains exported transcripts\n   - Pros: Transcripts always available\n   - Cons: More complex, continuous I/O\n\n3. **Reference OpenCode DB**: Store pointer to OpenCode's SQLite database\n   - Pros: No duplication\n   - Cons: Entire CLI would need to parse OpenCode's DB format\n\n**Recommendation:** Option 1 (export on demand) for simplicity.\n\n### User Prompt Detection\n\nOpenCode doesn't have a direct \"user prompt submitted\" event.\n\n**Workaround Options:**\n\n1. Use `message.updated` event and filter for user messages\n2. Use `session.updated` and check if user message count increased\n3. Add custom TUI event when prompt is submitted (requires OpenCode core changes)\n4. Skip user-prompt-submit hook for OpenCode (less metadata but still functional)\n\n**Recommendation:** Option 1 or 4. The user-prompt-submit hook is primarily for metadata enrichment, not critical functionality.\n\n### Subagent Task Tracking\n\nOpenCode has a Task tool that spawns subagents, similar to Claude Code.\n\n**Mapping:**\n- `tool.execute.before` with `tool: \"task\"` → `pre-task` hook\n- `tool.execute.after` with `tool: \"task\"` → `post-task` hook\n- Task metadata passed in `input.args` and `output.metadata`\n\n**Agent ID extraction:** OpenCode's Task tool should return an `agentId` in the result. The plugin must extract this and pass it to the post-task hook.\n\n### Git Strategy Compatibility\n\nOpenCode doesn't have built-in git strategies like Entire CLI.\n\n**Integration:**\n- OpenCode plugin calls Entire CLI hooks\n- Entire CLI applies the configured strategy (manual-commit or auto-commit)\n- OpenCode benefits from shadow branches, metadata branches, rewind capabilities\n\n**User experience:**\n- User runs `entire setup` to configure strategy\n- User adds `opencode-entire-integration` plugin to OpenCode config\n- OpenCode sessions automatically get tracked via Entire's git integration\n- User can run `entire logs`, `entire rewind` to manage OpenCode sessions\n\n---\n\n## Performance & Scalability\n\n### Hook Execution Overhead\n\n**Current (Claude Code):**\n- Hook commands spawn Go process per event (~10-50ms overhead)\n- Synchronous git operations (tree builds, commits)\n- Acceptable for human-paced interaction\n\n**OpenCode Integration:**\n- Similar overhead per hook\n- OpenCode may generate more events (multi-agent, more tools)\n- Risk: Hook saturation if OpenCode generates hundreds of tool calls\n\n**Mitigation:**\n- Batch checkpoint updates where possible\n- Use incremental checkpoints (only save diffs)\n- Add hook debouncing (queue events, process batch)\n- Make hooks async where feasible (don't block OpenCode)\n\n### Storage Impact\n\nOpenCode sessions with multiple agents could generate:\n- Larger transcript files (multiple agents = more messages)\n- More checkpoint commits (more tool uses)\n- More task metadata directories\n\n**Mitigation:**\n- Entire's compaction (condensing to metadata branch) helps\n- Consider checkpoint pruning strategies\n- Monitor storage usage in testing\n\n---\n\n## Security Considerations\n\n### Command Injection\n\nPlugin spawns `entire hooks opencode <hook-name>` with JSON input.\n\n**Risk:** If session IDs or tool inputs contain shell metacharacters, could lead to injection.\n\n**Mitigation:**\n- Use Bun's `$` shell with proper quoting (it handles escaping)\n- Validate/sanitize inputs in plugin before spawning\n- Entire CLI should validate JSON input structure\n\n### Transcript Exposure\n\nTranscripts may contain sensitive data (API keys, credentials, file contents).\n\n**Current state:**\n- Entire CLI stores transcripts in `.entire/metadata/` (gitignored)\n- Metadata branch may be pushed to remote (contains transcripts)\n\n**Considerations:**\n- Document that metadata branch may contain sensitive data\n- Provide option to not push metadata branch\n- Add pre-push hook to warn about sensitive data\n\n### Plugin Installation\n\nNPM plugins run with full filesystem and network access.\n\n**Considerations:**\n- Plugin should be open source and auditable\n- Published to NPM with verified publisher\n- Users should review plugin code before installing\n- Consider code signing for additional trust\n\n---\n\n## Recommendations\n\n### Recommended Approach: OpenCode Plugin\n\nI recommend **Option 1 (OpenCode Plugin)** for the following reasons:\n\n1. **Minimal coupling:** OpenCode and Entire CLI remain independent\n2. **Leverages existing systems:** Uses OpenCode's native plugin architecture\n3. **Easier maintenance:** Plugin can be versioned and updated independently\n4. **Flexibility:** Users can choose to use Entire with OpenCode or not\n5. **Cleaner architecture:** Each system does what it's best at\n6. **Faster iteration:** Can develop and test plugin without rebuilding Entire CLI\n\n### Implementation Phases\n\n**Phase 1: MVP Plugin (Core Hooks)**\n- Implement session-start, stop hooks\n- Basic checkpoint creation (no task tracking)\n- Export transcripts to `.entire/opencode-sessions/`\n- Test with manual-commit strategy\n\n**Phase 2: Task Tracking**\n- Implement pre-task, post-task hooks\n- Link subagent transcripts to checkpoints\n- Test with OpenCode's multi-agent features\n\n**Phase 3: Enhanced Metadata**\n- Implement post-todo hook\n- Extract todo content for checkpoint messages\n- User prompt detection (message.updated events)\n\n**Phase 4: Polish & Optimization**\n- Hook debouncing/batching\n- Error handling and retry logic\n- Performance profiling\n- Documentation and examples\n\n### Testing Strategy\n\n1. **Unit tests:** Test plugin hooks in isolation with mocked OpenCode events\n2. **Integration tests:** Test plugin with real OpenCode and Entire CLI\n3. **Multi-agent tests:** Verify task tracking with nested agents\n4. **Strategy tests:** Test with both manual-commit and auto-commit strategies\n5. **Performance tests:** Measure overhead, identify bottlenecks\n6. **Storage tests:** Verify metadata storage, compaction, cleanup\n\n---\n\n## Open Questions\n\n1. **OpenCode session database access:** How should we extract transcript data? Direct DB access or via OpenCode API?\n\n2. **Agent type naming:** Should Entire CLI have explicit \"OpenCode\" agent type, or generic \"plugin-based\" agent type?\n\n3. **Hook naming conventions:** Use `entire hooks opencode` or `entire hooks openai-agent` or something else?\n\n4. **Plugin distribution:** NPM package, bundled with Entire CLI, or both?\n\n5. **Configuration:** Should users configure integration in `opencode.json`, `.entire/settings.json`, or both?\n\n6. **Backward compatibility:** If OpenCode changes event schemas, how do we handle version mismatches?\n\n7. **Multiple strategies:** Should OpenCode integration support both manual-commit and auto-commit, or recommend one?\n\n---\n\n## Next Steps\n\nSee the companion document `opencode-integration-plan.md` for detailed implementation steps, code samples, and timeline estimates.\n"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":318,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2006,"cache_read_input_tokens":60505,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2006,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":5063,"service_tier":"standard"}},"requestId":"req_011CWyZnuVDwFLTwozN7gsdM","type":"assistant","uuid":"b6396b80-3955-4870-902f-bd301b865fba","timestamp":"2026-01-10T10:50:25.810Z"}
